(66,1005,'enriquezblythe@gmail.com','Re:','SLIDE 1 â€“ TITLE PRESENTATION\nGood morning, respected panelists, our adviser, and everyone present today.\nWe are here to present our capstone project entitled HR Legal and Compliance Management Module, which is designed to be integrated into the existing Human Resource Management System of Bestlink College of the Philippines â€“ Bulacan Campus.\nOur project focuses on an important responsibility of every educational institution: ensuring that employee-related processes are properly documented, monitored, and aligned with Philippine labor laws and government requirements.\nAs we all know, educational institutions are not only responsible for providing quality education. They are also employers that must fulfill their legal responsibilities toward faculty members, administrative staff, and other employees.\nThese responsibilities include proper employment documentation, statutory contributions, workplace policies, employee concerns, and compliance with applicable labor regulations.\nThrough our proposed system, we aim to support the Human Resource Department in managing these responsibilities more efficiently.\n\nSLIDE 2 â€“ INTRODUCTION\nOur project is an HR Legal and Compliance Management Module that will be integrated into the existing HRMS of Bestlink College of the Philippines â€“ Bulacan Campus.\nBut before discussing our system, we would like to explain why legal and compliance management is important in an educational institution.\nIn the Philippines, employers are required to comply with various labor laws and government regulations.\nFor example, employers have responsibilities concerning SSS, PhilHealth, Pag-IBIG, and BIR requirements.\nThey must also observe applicable wage regulations, employment standards, and workplace policies.\nNow, let\'s relate this to the environment of Bestlink College of the Philippines â€“ Bulacan Campus.\nSince Bestlink is an educational institution, its Human Resource Department handles employment-related responsibilities involving different categories of employees, such as teaching personnel, administrative staff, and other school employees.\nEach employee may have different employment arrangements, documents, and applicable benefits.\nFor example, a newly hired instructor may need to submit employment requirements, sign an employment contract, and receive orientation regarding institutional policies.\nMeanwhile, an administrative employee may request a certificate of employment, report a workplace concern, or ask HR about statutory contributions.\nThe question is: How can HR efficiently monitor all these responsibilities, especially when information is maintained across different records?\nThis is where our proposed HR Legal and Compliance Management Module becomes useful.\nOur system aims to provide a centralized platform where authorized HR personnel can manage legal documents, monitor compliance requirements, track employee concerns, and generate reports.\nThe purpose is not to replace the HR Department but to provide a tool that supports its daily operations and compliance responsibilities.\n\nSLIDE 3 â€“ BACKGROUND OF THE CAPSTONE PROJECT: CURRENT SITUATION\nFor the current situation, our project focuses on the existing HR processes of Bestlink College of the Philippines â€“ Bulacan Campus.\nBased on the problem identified in our capstone study, legal and compliance-related activities are handled through existing HR processes and available records.\nHowever, there is no dedicated module specifically designed to centralize the monitoring of these activities.\nTo better understand this situation, let us consider a realistic example.\nSuppose the HR Department needs to verify whether an employee\'s government information and required employment documents are complete.\nHR may need to review several records to verify the employee\'s details and determine whether any documents are missing.\nAnother example is when HR needs to monitor the acknowledgement of a newly issued workplace policy.\nWithout a centralized monitoring feature, checking which employees have acknowledged the policy may require additional manual verification.\nThese situations may not necessarily mean that the institution is violating labor laws.\nHowever, they demonstrate how fragmented records can make compliance monitoring more time-consuming and difficult to manage.\nIn our project, we want to address this administrative challenge by providing one platform where authorized HR personnel can easily access and monitor relevant information.\n\nSLIDE 4 â€“ BACKGROUND: CHALLENGES\nThe main challenge addressed by our project is the difficulty of monitoring different HR legal and compliance activities when records and processes are separated.\nIn the Philippine setting, this is particularly important because employers have recurring government and labor-related obligations.\nFor example, SSS, PhilHealth, and Pag-IBIG contributions must be properly handled according to the applicable rules and deadlines.\nEmployers also have tax-related responsibilities involving employee compensation.\nImagine a situation where an HR or payroll employee needs to verify whether the necessary information for a government contribution report has been completed.\nIf the supporting records are stored separately, the employee may spend additional time gathering and checking the information.\nAnother challenge involves employee complaints.\nFor example, suppose a staff member raises a concern regarding overtime compensation or workplace treatment.\nThe HR Department needs to document the concern, review the relevant information, coordinate with the appropriate personnel, and monitor the action taken.\nWithout an organized tracking mechanism, following up on the complaint may become more difficult.\nOur system aims to address these challenges by centralizing records and providing monitoring and tracking functions.\n\nSLIDE 5 â€“ PROPOSED SOLUTION\nTo address the identified challenges, our group proposes the development of an HR Legal and Compliance Management Module.\nThe module will be integrated into the existing HRMS of Bestlink College of the Philippines â€“ Bulacan Campus.\nInstead of maintaining compliance-related information across different records, the system will provide a centralized platform for authorized personnel.\nFor example, if HR needs to monitor employee policy acknowledgements, the system can display which employees have completed the acknowledgement and which records still require attention.\nIf an employee submits a document request, HR can monitor its processing status.\nIf a workplace incident is reported, authorized personnel can record the incident, document the actions taken, and monitor its progress.\nThe system also includes notifications, reporting features, and Lala AI.\nLala AI is intended to assist users by providing guidance based on available Philippine labor law references and government issuances.\nHowever, it is important to clarify that Lala AI is only a support tool.\nIt does not replace legal professionals, official government interpretations, or the responsibility of HR personnel to verify applicable laws.\nOverall, our proposed solution aims to improve the organization, accessibility, and monitoring of HR legal and compliance activities.\n\nSLIDES 6â€“7 â€“ STATEMENT OF THE PROBLEM\nOur project identifies seven major problems.\nFirst is separate records.\nIn an educational institution, HR manages different types of records, including employment documents, institutional policies, employee complaints, and government compliance information.\nWhen these records are maintained separately, retrieving and verifying information may require additional effort.\nSecond is difficulty in monitoring compliance.\nFor example, the HR Department must consider applicable government contribution requirements, wage regulations, and employment-related obligations.\nUnder Philippine labor regulations, employers must comply with the wage orders applicable to their location and the employment classifications concerned.\nSince Bestlink Bulacan Campus is located in the City of San Jose del Monte, Bulacan, applicable regional wage requirements must be considered.\nOur system aims to support monitoring of these requirements, although HR must still verify the latest official regulations.\nThird is case and document tracking.\nFor example, an employee may submit a request for a certificate of employment or raise a concern regarding workplace conditions.\nThe system will help HR monitor the request or complaint from submission until its recorded resolution.\nFourth is manual document processing.\nConsider an employee requesting a certificate of employment for a bank application.\nInstead of repeatedly preparing the document from the beginning, the proposed module can help HR generate documents using available employee information.\nFifth is policy acknowledgement monitoring.\nFor example, when the institution updates its employee handbook or workplace policies, HR needs to monitor whether employees have received and acknowledged the updated policies.\nOur system will provide a function for recording these acknowledgements.\nSixth is external legal case monitoring.\nIf the institution becomes involved in an employment-related legal proceeding, authorized personnel may need to monitor case documents, schedules, and developments.\nOur system provides a dedicated function for organizing this information.\nLastly, report preparation and access to legal references.\nPreparing reports may become time-consuming when HR needs to collect information from several sources.\nOur system aims to simplify reporting while providing access to relevant labor law references.\n\nSLIDE 8 â€“ GOALS\nThe main goal of our project is to improve the management of HR legal and compliance activities through a centralized system.\nSpecifically, we aim to reduce the difficulty of organizing records, monitoring deadlines, tracking employee concerns, and preparing reports.\nFor example, suppose HR needs to identify employment contracts approaching their expiration dates.\nInstead of checking each record individually, the system can help identify contracts that require attention.\nAnother example involves incomplete employee documents.\nThe system can provide a summary of records that need to be completed or updated.\nThis can help HR personnel prioritize their work and address administrative concerns more efficiently.\nMost importantly, the system aims to support informed HR decision-making by providing accessible and organized information.\n\nSLIDES 9â€“10 â€“ OBJECTIVES\nOur objectives describe the specific functions that we intend to develop.\nOne major objective is to centralize legal and compliance records.\nThis means authorized personnel can manage relevant information through one integrated platform.\nAnother objective is to monitor government compliance requirements.\nFor example, HR can maintain records related to SSS, PhilHealth, Pag-IBIG, BIR, and applicable salary requirements.\nWe also aim to improve employee document management.\nThis includes document requests, document generation, verification, and status tracking.\nAnother objective is to manage workplace incidents and complaints.\nFor example, if an employee reports a workplace concern, the system can help HR document the report, record the investigation or action taken, and monitor the outcome.\nWe also aim to monitor employee separation requirements.\nFor instance, when an employee resigns, HR may need to coordinate the return of institutional property, completion of clearance requirements, and preparation of separation documents.\nThe system can help organize these activities.\nHowever, employee final pay and other statutory entitlements must still be handled according to applicable labor regulations.\nLastly, we aim to provide notifications, reporting features, and access to labor law guidance through Lala AI.\n\nSLIDES 11â€“12 â€“ SCOPE OF THE SYSTEM\nOur HR Legal and Compliance Management Module consists of ten major functional areas.\n1. Dashboard Overview\nThe dashboard provides HR personnel with an overview of compliance-related activities.\nFor example, HR can identify pending documents, upcoming deadlines, and records that require attention.\n2. Labor Law and Government Compliance\nThis feature supports monitoring of government-related requirements and relevant labor law references.\nFor example, HR may use this function to organize records relating to statutory contributions and applicable wage requirements.\nIt is important to clarify that our module is designed for monitoring and record management.\nIt does not automatically guarantee government compliance or replace official government reporting systems.\n3. Legal Documents and Policy Management\nThis feature manages employee documents, document requests, institutional policies, and employee acknowledgements.\nFor example, when an updated workplace policy is issued, HR can monitor its acknowledgement status.\n4. Incident and Complaint Management\nThis feature records workplace incidents and employee complaints.\nFor example, if an employee reports inappropriate workplace behavior, authorized HR personnel can document the concern and monitor the actions taken.\nBecause these records may contain sensitive information, access must be restricted to authorized personnel.\n5. Risk Assessment\nThis feature helps identify potential HR compliance risks.\nFor example, expired contracts, missing employee documents, or overdue administrative requirements may be flagged for review.\n6. Exit Acknowledgement\nThis feature helps HR monitor employee separation requirements.\nFor example, when a faculty member resigns, the system can track the submission of required documents and the return of institutional property.\n7. Legal Affairs\nThis feature supports the monitoring of external legal cases.\nFor example, if an employment-related case requires a scheduled hearing, authorized personnel can record the schedule and relevant case information.\n8. Email Communication\nThis feature provides pre-populated email content.\nFor example, HR can prepare a notification reminding an employee to complete a required document.\n9. Lala AI\nLala AI provides guidance based on available labor law references and government issuances.\nFor example, an HR user may ask about general rules concerning holiday pay or employee separation.\nThe system can provide reference-based guidance, subject to verification.\n10. Notifications and Reporting\nLastly, the system provides alerts and compliance-related reports.\nThese functions help HR monitor pending activities and prepare information for administrative review.\nTogether, these features aim to provide a more organized approach to HR legal and compliance management.\n\nSLIDES 13â€“14 â€“ METHODOLOGY\nFor our methodology, we used Agile Scrum.\nWe selected this methodology because our system contains multiple interconnected functions that require continuous development, testing, and improvement.\nInstead of developing the entire module at once, Agile Scrum allows us to divide the development process into smaller cycles called sprints.\nDuring the planning stage, we identified the requirements of the proposed module.\nWe then organized these requirements into a product backlog.\nDuring sprint planning, we selected the features to be developed.\nFor example, one development cycle may focus on compliance monitoring, while another may focus on document management or incident tracking.\nAfter developing each feature, we conduct testing to identify errors and verify whether the implemented functions meet the system requirements.\nWe then review the completed features and incorporate necessary improvements.\nThis approach is useful because legal and compliance management involves several processes that must work together.\nFor example, employee information stored in the existing HRMS may also be needed for document generation, compliance monitoring, and employee exit processing.\nThrough Agile Scrum, we can develop and improve these functions systematically.\n\nSLIDE 15 â€“ TECHNOLOGY STACKS\nFor our technology stack, we used PHP for backend development and MySQL for database management.\nWe also used HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and Bootstrap 5 for the user interface.\nThese technologies allow us to develop a web-based system that can be accessed through supported browsers.\nWe used PHPMailer to support email notifications and GitHub for source-code management and collaboration.\nThe module follows the interface structure of the existing HRMS.\nThis is important because our project is not intended to operate as a completely separate HR system.\nInstead, it is designed to integrate with the institution\'s existing HRMS structure.\n\nSLIDES 16â€“17 â€“ SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE\nOur system architecture is divided into six layers.\nThe first is the User Layer, which includes HR personnel, compliance personnel, and authorized administrators.\nThe second is the Presentation Layer, where users interact with the dashboard, forms, reports, and notifications.\nThe third is the Application Layer, which processes the functions of the module.\nThe fourth is the Integration and Security Layer.\nThis is particularly important because HR systems handle confidential employee information.\nFor example, employment records, incident reports, and legal documents should not be accessible to unauthorized users.\nOur proposed architecture includes role-based access control, session management, and audit trails.\nThe fifth is the Database Layer, which stores and connects relevant information.\nLastly, the External Services Layer supports functions such as email notifications.\nThese layers are designed to work together to support the system\'s operations.\n\nSLIDE 18 â€“ USE CASE DIAGRAM\nThis diagram presents the interaction between authorized users and the functions of our system.\nFor example, HR personnel may access compliance records, manage employee documents, monitor complaints, and generate reports.\nHowever, not every user should have access to every function.\nFor instance, an employee complaint may contain confidential information.\nTherefore, access to sensitive records should depend on the user\'s assigned role and permissions.\nThis is important in supporting employee privacy and responsible handling of HR information.\n\nSLIDES 19â€“22 â€“ BUSINESS PROCESS ARCHITECTURE\nOur Business Process Architecture illustrates how the proposed module supports different HR activities.\nThe processes include compliance monitoring, legal document management, employee complaints, risk assessment, exit acknowledgement, and legal affairs.\nOne important part is risk assessment.\nFor example, the system may identify records involving missing payroll information, expired contracts, incomplete employee documents, or pending clearances.\nThese records may indicate administrative concerns requiring HR review.\nHowever, identifying a risk does not automatically mean that a labor law violation has occurred.\nThe purpose of risk assessment is to help HR identify potential problems early and determine what actions may be necessary.\nThis supports a more preventive approach to compliance management.\n\nSLIDE 23 â€“ DATABASE SCHEMA\nOur database schema represents how information is organized and connected within the system.\nFor example, employee records may be connected to document requests, policy acknowledgements, incident reports, and compliance-related activities.\nBy establishing these relationships, the system can reduce unnecessary duplication and improve information retrieval.\nThis is especially important when multiple HR functions rely on the same employee information.\n\nSLIDE 24 â€“ DATA ARCHITECTURE\nOur data architecture explains how information is organized, stored, and accessed.\nThe module is designed to manage employee-related information and compliance records within a centralized database.\nBecause these records may contain personal and sensitive information, proper access restrictions and data handling practices are important.\nIn the Philippine setting, this is also relevant to Republic Act No. 10173, or the Data Privacy Act of 2012.\nTherefore, our system design considers the importance of protecting employee information and limiting access to authorized personnel.\n\nSLIDE 25 â€“ APPLICATION ARCHITECTURE\nOur application architecture illustrates how the different system functions interact.\nFor example, the document management feature may retrieve employee information from the existing HRMS.\nThe compliance monitoring feature may use relevant records to identify requirements needing attention.\nMeanwhile, the notification feature can inform authorized users about pending activities.\nThese interconnected functions are intended to make HR processes more organized and efficient.\n\nSLIDE 26 â€“ TECHNOLOGY ARCHITECTURE\nOur technology architecture presents the technical environment supporting the module.\nThe system uses web technologies, backend processing, database management, and server infrastructure.\nThe objective is to provide a system that can support authorized users while remaining compatible with the existing HRMS environment.\n\nSLIDES 27â€“28 â€“ SOFTWARE AND HARDWARE REQUIREMENTS\nFor the hardware requirements, our system is designed to operate using standard computer equipment.\nThe minimum specifications include an Intel Core i3 or equivalent processor, 4 GB RAM, and sufficient storage.\nHowever, we recommend higher specifications for better performance, especially when multiple users access the system.\nFor software requirements, the module uses PHP, MySQL, Apache, JavaScript, and other supporting technologies.\nIt can be accessed using modern web browsers.\nWe also consider security features such as authentication, role-based access control, and audit trails.\nThese are particularly important because HR legal and compliance records must be handled responsibly.\n\nFINAL SLIDE â€“ CONCLUSION\nTo conclude our presentation, our HR Legal and Compliance Management Module was developed to address the challenges of organizing, monitoring, and managing HR legal and compliance-related activities.\nIn the Philippine setting, educational institutions must comply with various employment regulations and government requirements.\nFor Bestlink College of the Philippines â€“ Bulacan Campus, our proposed module aims to support the Human Resource Department by providing centralized records, compliance monitoring, document management, incident tracking, risk assessment, legal case monitoring, and reporting functions.\nWe recognize that technology alone cannot guarantee legal compliance.\nCompliance still depends on accurate information, proper implementation of policies, responsible decision-making, and adherence to applicable laws.\nHowever, through our proposed module, we aim to provide HR personnel with a tool that can make compliance-related processes more organized, accessible, and easier to monitor.\nUltimately, our goal is to support more efficient HR operations while promoting responsible employment practices and better management of legal and compliance information.\nThat concludes our presentation.\nThank you, and magandang buhay!\nWe are now ready to answer your questions.\n\nOn 10/20/2026, 3:14:37 PM, Cheska Jalotjot Bautista wrote:','notification',NULL,0,'compliance','enriquezblythe@gmail.com','2026-10-20 15:14:36','2026-10-20 15:14:36'),
(67,1005,'jonhazucena@gmail.com','Re:','SLIDE 1 â€“ TITLE PRESENTATION\nGood morning, respected panelists, our adviser, and everyone present today.\nWe are here to present our capstone project entitled HR Legal and Compliance Management Module, which is designed to be integrated into the existing Human Resource Management System of Bestlink College of the Philippines â€“ Bulacan Campus.\nOur project focuses on an important responsibility of every educational institution: ensuring that employee-related processes are properly documented, monitored, and aligned with Philippine labor laws and government requirements.\nAs we all know, educational institutions are not only responsible for providing quality education. They are also employers that must fulfill their legal responsibilities toward faculty members, administrative staff, and other employees.\nThese responsibilities include proper employment documentation, statutory contributions, workplace policies, employee concerns, and compliance with applicable labor regulations.\nThrough our proposed system, we aim to support the Human Resource Department in managing these responsibilities more efficiently.\n\nSLIDE 2 â€“ INTRODUCTION\nOur project is an HR Legal and Compliance Management Module that will be integrated into the existing HRMS of Bestlink College of the Philippines â€“ Bulacan Campus.\nBut before discussing our system, we would like to explain why legal and compliance management is important in an educational institution.\nIn the Philippines, employers are required to comply with various labor laws and government regulations.\nFor example, employers have responsibilities concerning SSS, PhilHealth, Pag-IBIG, and BIR requirements.\nThey must also observe applicable wage regulations, employment standards, and workplace policies.\nNow, let\'s relate this to the environment of Bestlink College of the Philippines â€“ Bulacan Campus.\nSince Bestlink is an educational institution, its Human Resource Department handles employment-related responsibilities involving different categories of employees, such as teaching personnel, administrative staff, and other school employees.\nEach employee may have different employment arrangements, documents, and applicable benefits.\nFor example, a newly hired instructor may need to submit employment requirements, sign an employment contract, and receive orientation regarding institutional policies.\nMeanwhile, an administrative employee may request a certificate of employment, report a workplace concern, or ask HR about statutory contributions.\nThe question is: How can HR efficiently monitor all these responsibilities, especially when information is maintained across different records?\nThis is where our proposed HR Legal and Compliance Management Module becomes useful.\nOur system aims to provide a centralized platform where authorized HR personnel can manage legal documents, monitor compliance requirements, track employee concerns, and generate reports.\nThe purpose is not to replace the HR Department but to provide a tool that supports its daily operations and compliance responsibilities.\n\nSLIDE 3 â€“ BACKGROUND OF THE CAPSTONE PROJECT: CURRENT SITUATION\nFor the current situation, our project focuses on the existing HR processes of Bestlink College of the Philippines â€“ Bulacan Campus.\nBased on the problem identified in our capstone study, legal and compliance-related activities are handled through existing HR processes and available records.\nHowever, there is no dedicated module specifically designed to centralize the monitoring of these activities.\nTo better understand this situation, let us consider a realistic example.\nSuppose the HR Department needs to verify whether an employee\'s government information and required employment documents are complete.\nHR may need to review several records to verify the employee\'s details and determine whether any documents are missing.\nAnother example is when HR needs to monitor the acknowledgement of a newly issued workplace policy.\nWithout a centralized monitoring feature, checking which employees have acknowledged the policy may require additional manual verification.\nThese situations may not necessarily mean that the institution is violating labor laws.\nHowever, they demonstrate how fragmented records can make compliance monitoring more time-consuming and difficult to manage.\nIn our project, we want to address this administrative challenge by providing one platform where authorized HR personnel can easily access and monitor relevant information.\n\nSLIDE 4 â€“ BACKGROUND: CHALLENGES\nThe main challenge addressed by our project is the difficulty of monitoring different HR legal and compliance activities when records and processes are separated.\nIn the Philippine setting, this is particularly important because employers have recurring government and labor-related obligations.\nFor example, SSS, PhilHealth, and Pag-IBIG contributions must be properly handled according to the applicable rules and deadlines.\nEmployers also have tax-related responsibilities involving employee compensation.\nImagine a situation where an HR or payroll employee needs to verify whether the necessary information for a government contribution report has been completed.\nIf the supporting records are stored separately, the employee may spend additional time gathering and checking the information.\nAnother challenge involves employee complaints.\nFor example, suppose a staff member raises a concern regarding overtime compensation or workplace treatment.\nThe HR Department needs to document the concern, review the relevant information, coordinate with the appropriate personnel, and monitor the action taken.\nWithout an organized tracking mechanism, following up on the complaint may become more difficult.\nOur system aims to address these challenges by centralizing records and providing monitoring and tracking functions.\n\nSLIDE 5 â€“ PROPOSED SOLUTION\nTo address the identified challenges, our group proposes the development of an HR Legal and Compliance Management Module.\nThe module will be integrated into the existing HRMS of Bestlink College of the Philippines â€“ Bulacan Campus.\nInstead of maintaining compliance-related information across different records, the system will provide a centralized platform for authorized personnel.\nFor example, if HR needs to monitor employee policy acknowledgements, the system can display which employees have completed the acknowledgement and which records still require attention.\nIf an employee submits a document request, HR can monitor its processing status.\nIf a workplace incident is reported, authorized personnel can record the incident, document the actions taken, and monitor its progress.\nThe system also includes notifications, reporting features, and Lala AI.\nLala AI is intended to assist users by providing guidance based on available Philippine labor law references and government issuances.\nHowever, it is important to clarify that Lala AI is only a support tool.\nIt does not replace legal professionals, official government interpretations, or the responsibility of HR personnel to verify applicable laws.\nOverall, our proposed solution aims to improve the organization, accessibility, and monitoring of HR legal and compliance activities.\n\nSLIDES 6â€“7 â€“ STATEMENT OF THE PROBLEM\nOur project identifies seven major problems.\nFirst is separate records.\nIn an educational institution, HR manages different types of records, including employment documents, institutional policies, employee complaints, and government compliance information.\nWhen these records are maintained separately, retrieving and verifying information may require additional effort.\nSecond is difficulty in monitoring compliance.\nFor example, the HR Department must consider applicable government contribution requirements, wage regulations, and employment-related obligations.\nUnder Philippine labor regulations, employers must comply with the wage orders applicable to their location and the employment classifications concerned.\nSince Bestlink Bulacan Campus is located in the City of San Jose del Monte, Bulacan, applicable regional wage requirements must be considered.\nOur system aims to support monitoring of these requirements, although HR must still verify the latest official regulations.\nThird is case and document tracking.\nFor example, an employee may submit a request for a certificate of employment or raise a concern regarding workplace conditions.\nThe system will help HR monitor the request or complaint from submission until its recorded resolution.\nFourth is manual document processing.\nConsider an employee requesting a certificate of employment for a bank application.\nInstead of repeatedly preparing the document from the beginning, the proposed module can help HR generate documents using available employee information.\nFifth is policy acknowledgement monitoring.\nFor example, when the institution updates its employee handbook or workplace policies, HR needs to monitor whether employees have received and acknowledged the updated policies.\nOur system will provide a function for recording these acknowledgements.\nSixth is external legal case monitoring.\nIf the institution becomes involved in an employment-related legal proceeding, authorized personnel may need to monitor case documents, schedules, and developments.\nOur system provides a dedicated function for organizing this information.\nLastly, report preparation and access to legal references.\nPreparing reports may become time-consuming when HR needs to collect information from several sources.\nOur system aims to simplify reporting while providing access to relevant labor law references.\n\nSLIDE 8 â€“ GOALS\nThe main goal of our project is to improve the management of HR legal and compliance activities through a centralized system.\nSpecifically, we aim to reduce the difficulty of organizing records, monitoring deadlines, tracking employee concerns, and preparing reports.\nFor example, suppose HR needs to identify employment contracts approaching their expiration dates.\nInstead of checking each record individually, the system can help identify contracts that require attention.\nAnother example involves incomplete employee documents.\nThe system can provide a summary of records that need to be completed or updated.\nThis can help HR personnel prioritize their work and address administrative concerns more efficiently.\nMost importantly, the system aims to support informed HR decision-making by providing accessible and organized information.\n\nSLIDES 9â€“10 â€“ OBJECTIVES\nOur objectives describe the specific functions that we intend to develop.\nOne major objective is to centralize legal and compliance records.\nThis means authorized personnel can manage relevant information through one integrated platform.\nAnother objective is to monitor government compliance requirements.\nFor example, HR can maintain records related to SSS, PhilHealth, Pag-IBIG, BIR, and applicable salary requirements.\nWe also aim to improve employee document management.\nThis includes document requests, document generation, verification, and status tracking.\nAnother objective is to manage workplace incidents and complaints.\nFor example, if an employee reports a workplace concern, the system can help HR document the report, record the investigation or action taken, and monitor the outcome.\nWe also aim to monitor employee separation requirements.\nFor instance, when an employee resigns, HR may need to coordinate the return of institutional property, completion of clearance requirements, and preparation of separation documents.\nThe system can help organize these activities.\nHowever, employee final pay and other statutory entitlements must still be handled according to applicable labor regulations.\nLastly, we aim to provide notifications, reporting features, and access to labor law guidance through Lala AI.\n\nSLIDES 11â€“12 â€“ SCOPE OF THE SYSTEM\nOur HR Legal and Compliance Management Module consists of ten major functional areas.\n1. Dashboard Overview\nThe dashboard provides HR personnel with an overview of compliance-related activities.\nFor example, HR can identify pending documents, upcoming deadlines, and records that require attention.\n2. Labor Law and Government Compliance\nThis feature supports monitoring of government-related requirements and relevant labor law references.\nFor example, HR may use this function to organize records relating to statutory contributions and applicable wage requirements.\nIt is important to clarify that our module is designed for monitoring and record management.\nIt does not automatically guarantee government compliance or replace official government reporting systems.\n3. Legal Documents and Policy Management\nThis feature manages employee documents, document requests, institutional policies, and employee acknowledgements.\nFor example, when an updated workplace policy is issued, HR can monitor its acknowledgement status.\n4. Incident and Complaint Management\nThis feature records workplace incidents and employee complaints.\nFor example, if an employee reports inappropriate workplace behavior, authorized HR personnel can document the concern and monitor the actions taken.\nBecause these records may contain sensitive information, access must be restricted to authorized personnel.\n5. Risk Assessment\nThis feature helps identify potential HR compliance risks.\nFor example, expired contracts, missing employee documents, or overdue administrative requirements may be flagged for review.\n6. Exit Acknowledgement\nThis feature helps HR monitor employee separation requirements.\nFor example, when a faculty member resigns, the system can track the submission of required documents and the return of institutional property.\n7. Legal Affairs\nThis feature supports the monitoring of external legal cases.\nFor example, if an employment-related case requires a scheduled hearing, authorized personnel can record the schedule and relevant case information.\n8. Email Communication\nThis feature provides pre-populated email content.\nFor example, HR can prepare a notification reminding an employee to complete a required document.\n9. Lala AI\nLala AI provides guidance based on available labor law references and government issuances.\nFor example, an HR user may ask about general rules concerning holiday pay or employee separation.\nThe system can provide reference-based guidance, subject to verification.\n10. Notifications and Reporting\nLastly, the system provides alerts and compliance-related reports.\nThese functions help HR monitor pending activities and prepare information for administrative review.\nTogether, these features aim to provide a more organized approach to HR legal and compliance management.\n\nSLIDES 13â€“14 â€“ METHODOLOGY\nFor our methodology, we used Agile Scrum.\nWe selected this methodology because our system contains multiple interconnected functions that require continuous development, testing, and improvement.\nInstead of developing the entire module at once, Agile Scrum allows us to divide the development process into smaller cycles called sprints.\nDuring the planning stage, we identified the requirements of the proposed module.\nWe then organized these requirements into a product backlog.\nDuring sprint planning, we selected the features to be developed.\nFor example, one development cycle may focus on compliance monitoring, while another may focus on document management or incident tracking.\nAfter developing each feature, we conduct testing to identify errors and verify whether the implemented functions meet the system requirements.\nWe then review the completed features and incorporate necessary improvements.\nThis approach is useful because legal and compliance management involves several processes that must work together.\nFor example, employee information stored in the existing HRMS may also be needed for document generation, compliance monitoring, and employee exit processing.\nThrough Agile Scrum, we can develop and improve these functions systematically.\n\nSLIDE 15 â€“ TECHNOLOGY STACKS\nFor our technology stack, we used PHP for backend development and MySQL for database management.\nWe also used HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and Bootstrap 5 for the user interface.\nThese technologies allow us to develop a web-based system that can be accessed through supported browsers.\nWe used PHPMailer to support email notifications and GitHub for source-code management and collaboration.\nThe module follows the interface structure of the existing HRMS.\nThis is important because our project is not intended to operate as a completely separate HR system.\nInstead, it is designed to integrate with the institution\'s existing HRMS structure.\n\nSLIDES 16â€“17 â€“ SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE\nOur system architecture is divided into six layers.\nThe first is the User Layer, which includes HR personnel, compliance personnel, and authorized administrators.\nThe second is the Presentation Layer, where users interact with the dashboard, forms, reports, and notifications.\nThe third is the Application Layer, which processes the functions of the module.\nThe fourth is the Integration and Security Layer.\nThis is particularly important because HR systems handle confidential employee information.\nFor example, employment records, incident reports, and legal documents should not be accessible to unauthorized users.\nOur proposed architecture includes role-based access control, session management, and audit trails.\nThe fifth is the Database Layer, which stores and connects relevant information.\nLastly, the External Services Layer supports functions such as email notifications.\nThese layers are designed to work together to support the system\'s operations.\n\nSLIDE 18 â€“ USE CASE DIAGRAM\nThis diagram presents the interaction between authorized users and the functions of our system.\nFor example, HR personnel may access compliance records, manage employee documents, monitor complaints, and generate reports.\nHowever, not every user should have access to every function.\nFor instance, an employee complaint may contain confidential information.\nTherefore, access to sensitive records should depend on the user\'s assigned role and permissions.\nThis is important in supporting employee privacy and responsible handling of HR information.\n\nSLIDES 19â€“22 â€“ BUSINESS PROCESS ARCHITECTURE\nOur Business Process Architecture illustrates how the proposed module supports different HR activities.\nThe processes include compliance monitoring, legal document management, employee complaints, risk assessment, exit acknowledgement, and legal affairs.\nOne important part is risk assessment.\nFor example, the system may identify records involving missing payroll information, expired contracts, incomplete employee documents, or pending clearances.\nThese records may indicate administrative concerns requiring HR review.\nHowever, identifying a risk does not automatically mean that a labor law violation has occurred.\nThe purpose of risk assessment is to help HR identify potential problems early and determine what actions may be necessary.\nThis supports a more preventive approach to compliance management.\n\nSLIDE 23 â€“ DATABASE SCHEMA\nOur database schema represents how information is organized and connected within the system.\nFor example, employee records may be connected to document requests, policy acknowledgements, incident reports, and compliance-related activities.\nBy establishing these relationships, the system can reduce unnecessary duplication and improve information retrieval.\nThis is especially important when multiple HR functions rely on the same employee information.\n\nSLIDE 24 â€“ DATA ARCHITECTURE\nOur data architecture explains how information is organized, stored, and accessed.\nThe module is designed to manage employee-related information and compliance records within a centralized database.\nBecause these records may contain personal and sensitive information, proper access restrictions and data handling practices are important.\nIn the Philippine setting, this is also relevant to Republic Act No. 10173, or the Data Privacy Act of 2012.\nTherefore, our system design considers the importance of protecting employee information and limiting access to authorized personnel.\n\nSLIDE 25 â€“ APPLICATION ARCHITECTURE\nOur application architecture illustrates how the different system functions interact.\nFor example, the document management feature may retrieve employee information from the existing HRMS.\nThe compliance monitoring feature may use relevant records to identify requirements needing attention.\nMeanwhile, the notification feature can inform authorized users about pending activities.\nThese interconnected functions are intended to make HR processes more organized and efficient.\n\nSLIDE 26 â€“ TECHNOLOGY ARCHITECTURE\nOur technology architecture presents the technical environment supporting the module.\nThe system uses web technologies, backend processing, database management, and server infrastructure.\nThe objective is to provide a system that can support authorized users while remaining compatible with the existing HRMS environment.\n\nSLIDES 27â€“28 â€“ SOFTWARE AND HARDWARE REQUIREMENTS\nFor the hardware requirements, our system is designed to operate using standard computer equipment.\nThe minimum specifications include an Intel Core i3 or equivalent processor, 4 GB RAM, and sufficient storage.\nHowever, we recommend higher specifications for better performance, especially when multiple users access the system.\nFor software requirements, the module uses PHP, MySQL, Apache, JavaScript, and other supporting technologies.\nIt can be accessed using modern web browsers.\nWe also consider security features such as authentication, role-based access control, and audit trails.\nThese are particularly important because HR legal and compliance records must be handled responsibly.\n\nFINAL SLIDE â€“ CONCLUSION\nTo conclude our presentation, our HR Legal and Compliance Management Module was developed to address the challenges of organizing, monitoring, and managing HR legal and compliance-related activities.\nIn the Philippine setting, educational institutions must comply with various employment regulations and government requirements.\nFor Bestlink College of the Philippines â€“ Bulacan Campus, our proposed module aims to support the Human Resource Department by providing centralized records, compliance monitoring, document management, incident tracking, risk assessment, legal case monitoring, and reporting functions.\nWe recognize that technology alone cannot guarantee legal compliance.\nCompliance still depends on accurate information, proper implementation of policies, responsible decision-making, and adherence to applicable laws.\nHowever, through our proposed module, we aim to provide HR personnel with a tool that can make compliance-related processes more organized, accessible, and easier to monitor.\nUltimately, our goal is to support more efficient HR operations while promoting responsible employment practices and better management of legal and compliance information.\nThat concludes our presentation.\nThank you, and magandang buhay!\nWe are now ready to answer your questions.\n\nOn 10/20/2026, 3:14:37 PM, Cheska Jalotjot Bautista wrote:','notification',NULL,0,'compliance','enriquezblythe@gmail.com','2026-10-20 15:14:36','2026-10-20 15:14:36'),
